-// follows the call, so releasing a long chain of cells (a list) recursed
-// once per cell and could overflow the stack. No token produced inside drop
-// glue can be reused, so there a release of a plain shared box whose type
-// can form a chain calls the type's `drop_and_free` instead (only once its
-// count was read as 1). Per arm, it drops the cell's other managed members,
-// deallocates the cell, and then releases its chain members: the boxes of
-// its own recursive group (else its last managed member, if that is such a
-// box). Of those, the last one that is about to be freed (count 1) is
-// released last, as a tail call, so a chain is freed in a loop whichever
-// member carries it; the others are released first. Releases outside drop
-// glue (whose tokens may be reused) are unchanged.
+// Drop glue frees the record cells a freed value holds through one stack of
+// pending releases per thread (the runtime's `__reussir_drop_defer` and
+// `__reussir_drop_drain`), as Lean's `lean_del` does with its to-do list, so
+// freeing a value deep through records takes bounded stack. No token
+// produced inside drop glue can be reused, so there a member that is a plain
+// shared record box is, when its count is 1, deferred with its type's
+// `drop_and_free` (else decremented); other members are released as before.
+// `drop_in_place` (the glue entry: it drops a record's contents) drains the
+// stack before returning. `drop_and_free`, which only ever runs from that
+// drain, frees a cell whose count was read as 1: per arm it releases the
+// members (deferring the record boxes) but the last record box, frees the
+// cell, and then releases that last box directly, by a tail call when its
+// count is 1, so a chain is a loop. Releases outside drop glue (whose tokens
+// may be reused) are unchanged.
 
